hat text and  then you finally get a nice-looking  Arabic as for how the Unicode  bidirectionality algorithm works so  Arabic is a right to left language so  the Unicode by des algorithm specifies a  base direction or a line direction and  this is like this bottom long red line  here specifies the direction that the  text will flow in when it's rendered as  the user is typing it and then for every  single word I has its own direction so  this word this line right here says Bank  Bank on 2018 in New York City and the  terms Bank Bank on obviously flow from  right to left because they're every  keywords but in Arabic we read numbers  from left to right so the visual cursor  as the user is typing in a number has to  start flowing from the left to the right  then when the user types in Arabic word  again the cursor the visual person needs  to jump back to the end of the line and  start flowing from right to left and if  they type in English word it starts to  flow again from left to right so I was  about to like implement my own ever  consider but it's just like this is like  it was out of scope because there's a  lot of rules to handling by eye but as  you know it's good to learn and then the  other thing that you have to do when  handling Arabic text is that you have to  reshape it so Arabic letters don't take  the same form as they do in English like  you know you have in Arabic letters have  to be connected and they take a  different shape depending on where  they're at in the word um so in this top  line I have the letter the Arabic letter  h h h space h and the way this actually  gets displayed is like so below so the  letter h looks like this when it's in  the initial state looks like this when  it's in the middle state and then it has  a different look in the final state and  you have to reshape it and uh there's  more to reshaping than just simply like  looking up the letter and where it  belongs there's also these things as  ligatures Arabic certain Arabic  li